# nutz-verify — optional, and only for running this repo's tests.
#
# THE VERIFIER ITSELF NEEDS NONE OF THIS. It takes endpoints on the command line:
#
#   nutz-verify epoch 497063 --rpc https://rpc.mainnet.chain.robinhood.com
#
# That endpoint needs no key, and nothing about running the binary requires a credential, a
# config file or an account. ADR-0002's whole claim is that a hostile stranger downloads the
# binary and checks our arithmetic; a Verifier you had to be given access to would not be one.
#
# Copy to .env (gitignored) only if you are working on this repo.

# The anvil harness forks chain 4663 from here. Defaults to nothing, which degrades to a bare
# anvil — every decoder is still exercised, but the subtests that ask what 4663 itself serves
# skip. The public endpoint below is enough; no archive access is needed, because the harness
# deploys its own contracts and only ever reads block headers from before the fork.
NUTZ_VERIFY_FORK_RPC=https://rpc.mainnet.chain.robinhood.com

# The live tests read real 4663 history: NUTZ_VERIFY_LIVE_RPC alone exercises paging against
# the public endpoint's 10,000-result cap, and a second, independent provider turns on the
# cross-check that ADR-0002 leans on. Any 4663 endpoint works as the second one; an archive
# endpoint is only needed for deep history.
NUTZ_VERIFY_LIVE_RPC=https://rpc.mainnet.chain.robinhood.com
# NUTZ_VERIFY_LIVE_RPC_2=

# Turn a skipped harness into a failing one. CI sets both; a contributor without foundry
# installed wants neither.
#   NUTZ_VERIFY_REQUIRE_ANVIL=1   anvil, cast and a built nutz-contracts must be present
#   NUTZ_VERIFY_REQUIRE_FORK=1    ...and the node must really be a fork of 4663

# Where the sibling nutz-contracts checkout is, if it is not ../nutz-contracts. The harness
# needs its compiled artifacts: `forge build` there first.
# NUTZ_CONTRACTS=
